* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    deffun voc_guard(f) calling '!voc_guard'
*    if not(voc_guard(file.var)) then stop
*
* Owner's rulings 12 and 14 (Solo for Windows, 25 Sep 2026): a DIRECT edit of
* the VOC needs the administrator password (ADMIN).  op_dio3.c refuses a user
* program's own write, delete or clear of a VOC in C; the verbs that edit a
* file the user names (ED, EDIT, COPY, COPYP, DELETE, CLEAR.FILE, and
* DELETE.FILE and RENAME when the VOC itself is the target, and since 18 Oct
* 26 also GET.LIST DELETING, SAVE.LIST, DELETE.LIST, SORT.LIST and COPY.LIST
* with IN / FROM / TO, MAP FILE, FORMAT, FIXDIR, SP.VIEW, GENERATE, CD DATA,
* SET.TRIGGER, REFORMAT, PAGESEQ, CREATE.FILE on an existing file, COMO IN and
* BASIC's object file) are $internal,
* which the C test lets through, so they ask this instead.  The rule itself is
* !voc_refusal's, which displays nothing (the API server uses it).
*
* IT LOOKS AT THE FILE THAT WAS OPENED, NOT AT THE NAME THAT WAS TYPED, so a
* Q-pointer to a VOC, or "voc" in any case, is caught.  A VOC is a DH (dynamic)
* file whose last path component is "voc" - the same rule as
* voc_write_refused() in C.  The dictionary, voc.dic, is not the VOC.
*
* Returns @true when the edit may go ahead: not a VOC, or ADMIN is on.  On
* @false it has already said why (11008).
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function voc_guard(f)
$catalog !voc_guard

   deffun voc_refusal(f) calling '!voc_refusal'   ;* 18 Oct 26, Solo carry A

*  The rule (the VOC needs ADMIN; gcat, global.bp.out and solo.policy need a global-password
*  session, ADMIN not being enough) is !voc_refusal's.
   n = voc_refusal(f)
   if n = 0 then return @true

   display sysmsg(n) ;* 11008 The VOC can only be changed after ADMIN / 11028 The global catalogue can only be changed by the SD Core server
   return @false
end

* END-CODE
